## Example

For the one-vertex, one-loop quiver with loop $x$, take the potential
$W=x^2$. In dimension one, the representation space is $\mathbb C$, the
group $\operatorname{GL}_1$ acts trivially, and the trace function is
$W_1(z)=z^2$. Its critical locus is the origin, with critical value zero.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** the one-loop quiver, the potential $W=x^2$, and the local Milnor-fiber normalization in [[def-quiver-potential-and-monodromic-vanishing-cycle-data]].

[F1] [[def-quiver-potential-and-monodromic-vanishing-cycle-data]]: the dimension-one trace of a loop potential is its value on the scalar endomorphism, and an isolated critical point has vanishing-cycle stalk given by reduced cohomology of its local Milnor fiber, with monodromy from a positive loop.

[F2] [[lem-quiver-potential-trace-is-additive-on-extensions]]: the trace of the potential on an extension is the sum of its traces on the subrepresentation and quotient.

[F3] [[thm-normalized-rational-vanishing-cycles-have-coherent-operations]]: the exact standard normalization is $H^k(V_f\mathbb Q)_x=\widetilde H^{k-1}(F_x;\mathbb Q)$ and $V_0\mathbb Q=\mathbb Q$; positive-loop continuation gives its monodromy.


## Verification

1.1 The trace definition in [F1] gives $W_1(z)=z^2$. Its differential is $dW_1=2z\,dz$; since the coefficient field has characteristic zero, this vanishes exactly at $z=0$, where $W_1(0)=0$. Thus the critical locus and critical value are as stated. [F1, given, algebra]

2.1 For any sufficiently small $\epsilon\ne0$, the local Milnor fiber $z^2=\epsilon$ in a disk about zero consists of the two distinct points $\sqrt\epsilon$ and $-\sqrt\epsilon$. Its reduced zeroth cohomology is $\mathbb Q$, generated by the difference of the two point classes. When $\epsilon$ goes once around zero, the two roots exchange, so this generator maps to its negative. The normalized vanishing-cycle stalk $\Phi$ is therefore one-dimensional in degree one, with monodromy $-1$, by [F3]. The homological coefficient is its normalized equivariant Verdier dual as in [F1]; it must not be confused with an unshifted Milnor cohomology stalk. [F1, F3, step 1.1, construct]

3.1 This local calculation agrees with the degree-one normalization of [F3] and, under Thom–Sebastiani, with degree two for $x^2+y^2$. Trace additivity [F2] gives the extension value convention. The full operation package is proved by its separate supplier; this example verifies the specified local fibre, degree and monodromy. [F2, F3, step 2.1] ∎
